\chapter{Case Study II: A Query DSL}
\label{ch:case-query}\label{c39-case-study-ii-a-query-dsl}

\section{The problem: optimize a query without changing its
rows}\label{c39-the-problem-optimize-a-query-without-changing-its-rows}

The second case study builds an embedded query language over an
in-memory collection of people. The query selects rows whose age is at
least eighteen and projects their names. Its initial AST contains a
redundant conjunction with true. A rewrite set removes that redundancy,
and an independent interpreter evaluates both the original and rewritten
trees on the same data. The central obligation is semantic preservation,
not merely producing a shorter diagnostic string.

The language is SQL-style in its division into source, filter, and
projection, but it is not an SQL parser or SQL database implementation.
There is no textual SQL grammar, query planner, join algorithm,
null-value model, or database connection. Calling it an embedded query
DSL is precise: C++ factory calls construct a domain-specific AST whose
interpretation is supplied by the application.

DSLtk contributes the runtime-tagged tree representation and bottom-up
rewrite engine. The application defines a closed vocabulary, an
interpreter, a row type, and the laws that justify simplification. This
separation is useful because it makes the proof obligation visible. A
generic rewrite engine cannot know that removing a true predicate
preserves this particular interpreter's result; the domain design must
establish that fact.

\section{The query vocabulary}\label{c39-the-query-vocabulary}

The root tag \code{project-name} has one child: a relation. A
\code{source} leaf names the only available relation, \code{people}. A
\code{filter} node has a predicate and a relation child. Predicates
include the true leaf, a two-child conjunction, and \code{age-ge} with
one integer leaf. Integer leaves store text because that is the AST
representation's payload model.

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
Tag & Shape & Meaning \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
\code{source} & Leaf with value \code{people} & All input rows \\
\code{filter} & Predicate, relation & Keep rows satisfying the
predicate \\
\code{project-name} & One relation & Return each retained name \\
\code{true} & Leaf & Boolean true \\
\code{and} & Two predicates & Short-circuit conjunction \\
\code{age-ge} & One integer leaf & Compare row age to the bound \\
\code{integer} & Textual leaf & Checked integer constant \\
\end{longtable}
}

The tree is deliberately untyped at the C++ representation level. Every
factory returns \code{ASTNode}, so the interpreter must validate tags
and arities. The language vocabulary is closed: an unknown tag or
malformed shape throws instead of being silently ignored. This turns
accidental AST construction errors into observable failures at
interpretation time.

The integer reader uses \code{from_chars} and checks both its error code
and that the entire payload was consumed. A string beginning with digits
followed by junk is not a valid integer constant. This mirrors the units
case study's whole-spelling discipline, but uses a conversion API whose
end pointer makes the check explicit rather than a separate regex.

\section{The interpreter defines the
language}\label{c39-the-interpreter-defines-the-language}

The predicate interpreter receives a node and one \code{Person}. A true
leaf returns true. A conjunction evaluates its left child and then its
right only when needed. An age comparison reads the bound and compares
it to the row's integer age. No predicate mutates the row, writes
output, or changes external state. Purity is part of the language
contract and supports the rewrite law.

The relation interpreter copies the source rows or filters the result of
a child relation. Filtering preserves the input order. The projection
interpreter then collects names in that same order. This is a simple
semantic model, not a streaming execution engine. Copying relation
vectors makes ownership easy to reason about at the cost of intermediate
storage.

An optimization claim must refer to this exact model. A different
interpreter with side-effecting predicates, unordered relations, or
three-valued null logic could need different laws. The AST's tag
spelling does not prove that all familiar SQL identities hold. The
implementation defines what \code{and} and \code{filter} mean here, and
the rewrite set is chosen accordingly.

\section{The two simplification
rules}\label{c39-the-two-simplification-rules}

The first rule recognizes \texttt{and(true,p)} and replaces it with
\code{p}. Its predicate checks the node tag, two-child arity, and the
left child's leaf status and tag before accessing the replacement child.
The rule name is \code{and-true}; it is a debugging identifier rather
than a tag-dispatch mechanism. The actual match is entirely the user
predicate's work.

The second rule recognizes \texttt{filter(true,r)} and replaces it with
\code{r}. Although the principal demonstration simplifies a conjunction
inside a filter rather than removing the filter itself, the second rule
shows how local Boolean normalization can enable a higher-level
relational simplification. The bottom-up traversal gives a parent the
already simplified children when its own rules are tried.

Both laws preserve the interpreter's behavior because true is pure and
the interpreter uses ordinary Boolean conjunction. They also decrease
the number of nodes for the matched shapes. That decreasing measure
supplies a simple termination argument for this small rule set on finite
valid trees. The engine's pass budget remains an operational bound, but
these particular rules do not need it to avoid a rewrite cycle.

\section{Why rule predicates must be total on ordinary
inputs}\label{c39-why-rule-predicates-must-be-total-on-ordinary-inputs}

A predicate that calls \texttt{child(1)} before checking arity can throw
on a malformed or unrelated node. The rewrite engine visits every node,
not only the nodes the rule author had in mind. A good rule predicate
first checks the broad shape and only then inspects children. Its
failure should normally mean not applicable, not an exception caused by
an unchecked index.

That does not eliminate the need for full language validation. A
structurally matching \code{and} node could contain an invalid predicate
on its right. Replacing \texttt{and(true,invalid)} by \code{invalid}
preserves the eventual rejection behavior in this interpreter, but it
does not make the input a valid query. Validation and optimization are
separate passes with different responsibilities.

The case study keeps the interpreter's rejection policy explicit so that
optimization cannot accidentally turn an unknown construct into silent
success. A larger compiler would often validate the AST once before
optimization and then require each optimization to preserve a stronger
well-formedness invariant. The small example shows where that invariant
would be introduced.

\section{The complete implementation}\label{c39-the-complete-implementation}

The program is stored in \code{projects/query/main.cpp}. Its source is
included directly below. Build with \code{make build/query}; the same
target is included in \code{make examples}, which runs all five case
studies.

\lstinputlisting[style=dslcpp,caption={Case study II: query interpretation and semantics-preserving AST simplification.},label={lst:case-query}]{projects/query/main.cpp}

The data set contains Ada aged thirty-six, Lin aged seventeen, and Grace
aged eighty-five. The query's threshold is eighteen. The expected output
therefore contains Ada and Grace in their original order. Lin is
excluded. The program compares original and optimized results, checks
the exact expected names, and verifies that applying the rewrite set
again leaves the optimized tree structurally unchanged.

That final check is idempotence on this example, not a universal
confluence proof for arbitrary rewrite sets. It is nevertheless useful
evidence that the demonstrated tree reached the intended stable form.
The decreasing node-count argument explains why these two rules
terminate; the concrete idempotence check catches implementation
mistakes in constructing or applying them.

\section{Observed execution}\label{c39-observed-execution}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Notation, algorithm, or output}]
(project-name (filter (and (true) (age-ge (integer 18))) (source people)))
(project-name (filter (age-ge (integer 18)) (source people)))
Ada
Grace
\end{lstlisting}

The first line is the original AST, the second is the simplified AST,
and the remaining lines are interpreted results. The diagnostic dump
shows that the true conjunction disappeared while the age filter
remained. The result comparison shows that the change preserved the
selected rows for this data. Both observations are needed: a matching
output alone would not reveal that the optimization failed to run, and a
smaller tree alone would not establish correct selection.

The source also preserves the original tree because
\code{RewriteSet::apply} takes its root by value. This makes
before-and-after comparison natural. The cost is copying and rebuilding.
For a large query plan, a persistent or arena-based representation might
reduce copying, but that would introduce a different ownership model.
The current value model is a good fit for a small, inspectable
optimization case study.

\section{Relation to visitors and equality
saturation}\label{c39-relation-to-visitors-and-equality-saturation}

A visitor could collect query metrics, validate the tag vocabulary, or
produce a textual rendering. Its schema coverage would not replace the
interpreter's arity and payload checks. A \code{filter} tag being
covered by a handler does not prove that its two children have the
correct roles. The visitor and domain validator answer complementary
questions.

An e-graph could retain alternative equivalent query plans instead of
committing to one AST. That becomes interesting when pushdown, join
order, or projection placement interact. However, meaningful plan
selection would need a domain cost model, while the supplied extractor
minimizes node count. The simple rewrite set is a better fit for the
local true-elimination laws demonstrated here. Selecting a more
elaborate engine should follow the optimization problem rather than the
attractiveness of its name.

The first-order term engine is another possible representation, but it
would need an explicit encoding for relation and predicate sorts. The
current AST lets user predicates inspect arbitrary domain structure
directly. This is one of the advantages of the toolkit's several
transformation facilities: they support different problem shapes rather
than forcing every application through one universal rewriting
interface.

\section{Extending the language while preserving
meaning}\label{c39-extending-the-language-while-preserving-meaning}

Adding a second projected column changes the result type and projection
interpreter. Adding a new predicate changes the Boolean vocabulary and
may introduce new rewrite side conditions. Adding a join changes
relation semantics, row identity, and likely performance considerations.
Each extension should begin with an interpreter or executable
specification before optimization rules are added.

Null values are a particularly revealing extension. SQL-style null logic
is not ordinary two-valued Boolean logic, and comparisons can yield an
unknown result. Existing rules must be rechecked against the new truth
tables. Likewise, a predicate that invokes an external callback could
make evaluation order observable. The present laws rely on a pure,
two-valued model; their validity should not be transferred silently to a
richer language.

For testing, generate several small valid queries and compare original
and rewritten execution over varied row sets. Include empty relations,
boundary ages, repeated names, and predicates that are always true or
always false. This can reveal mistakes in rule orientation and tree
construction. It remains evidence rather than a formal proof, but an
independent interpreter provides a strong reference against which a
generic rewrite engine can be used responsibly.

The case study's reusable pattern is simple: define a closed vocabulary,
write its meaning directly, select laws justified by that meaning, and
compare before-and-after behavior. DSLtk supplies the mechanics for
constructing and rewriting the representation. The language designer
supplies the reason the rewrite is correct.

\section{A proof outline for the demonstrated
transformation}\label{c39-a-proof-outline-for-the-demonstrated-transformation}

The rewrite \texttt{and(true,p)} to \code{p} can be justified for each
row before reasoning about whole relations. If \code{p} evaluates to
true, the conjunction also evaluates to true. If it evaluates to false,
the conjunction evaluates to false. The removed true node has no
observable effect and cannot fail under the interpreter's definition.
Thus the replacement yields the same Boolean decision for every row on
which the predicate is defined.

Filtering applies that decision independently to rows in source order.
Since each decision is preserved, the same rows survive in the same
order. Projection reads the name field of each surviving row without
mutation. It follows that the final ordered sequence of names is
preserved. This reasoning connects the local Boolean law to the
observable query result; it does not rely only on the before-and-after
tree looking plausible.

The rule \texttt{filter(true,r)} to \code{r} has a similarly direct
argument: every row produced by the child relation is retained, in its
existing order. The intermediate vector allocation disappears from the
abstract evaluation path, but the returned value remains the same
sequence. Whether removing that allocation measurably improves a
particular program depends on how large the relation is and how the
compiler implements moves and elision. The semantic argument and the
performance hypothesis should be recorded separately.

Invalid trees need a separate discussion. The proof assumes the
vocabulary's well-formedness rules. It does not justify transforming
arbitrary malformed trees merely because some tags match. For example,
an application that reports all invalid descendants before
interpretation could observe a different diagnostic after a rewrite
removes a subtree. A compiler that promises stable validation errors
should validate before optimization, or explicitly define which
diagnostics may change after normalization.

\section{Testing beyond the three-row
example}\label{c39-testing-beyond-the-three-row-example}

The three-row data set provides a readable witness with both retained
and removed rows. A wider regression suite can systematically vary the
facts relevant to the threshold. Ages seventeen, eighteen, and nineteen
distinguish strict from non-strict comparison. An empty relation detects
assumptions about a first row. Two people with the same name ensure
projection preserves duplicates instead of accidentally converting the
result into a set.

Input order should be varied independently of the age distribution. If a
future interpreter sorts rows while filtering, comparing only sets of
names would miss the regression. The current language returns a vector,
so order is an observable part of its meaning. The reference comparison
should use that vector directly. If a future language deliberately
introduces unordered relations, its specification and tests should
change together.

Small query generation can cover nested conjunctions. Start from true
and age comparisons, combine them to a bounded depth, and place the
predicates inside filters. Evaluate each query before and after
rewriting over several small row sets. The generator should build only
valid trees for semantic equivalence tests; malformed-tree tests should
be a separate collection with explicit rejection expectations. Mixing
the two makes a thrown exception ambiguous rather than informative.

Repeated rewriting supplies another useful property: after reaching the
intended fixed point, a second application should preserve the
structure. This catches incomplete traversal or rules that rebuild an
equivalent but different shape on every pass. It does not by itself
establish that all possible rule orders reach the same result.
Confluence requires reasoning about overlaps between rules, and becomes
more important as the optimizer's vocabulary grows beyond strictly
simplifying identities.

\section{Planning an execution
representation}\label{c39-planning-an-execution-representation}

The AST is convenient for explaining and validating the query, but it
need not remain the representation used for every row. A later
compilation phase could translate the validated predicate into a
callable and evaluate that callable during filtering. This would move
tag and arity checks out of the inner loop. The resulting execution plan
would need to own its constants and clearly borrow or own its data
source.

Such a phase creates another correctness boundary. It should preserve
the interpreter's short-circuit order and integer-conversion policy, and
it should not retain references into a temporary AST. Comparing compiled
execution with the reference interpreter provides a direct test of the
lowering step. The interpreter remains useful even if the optimized
runtime eventually uses a very different representation.

For larger plans, distinguish the cost of constructing a query,
optimizing it, and executing it repeatedly. Expensive preparation may be
worthwhile for a reused query and wasteful for a one-shot filter over
three rows. DSLtk's generic tree and rewrite mechanics do not select
that lifecycle. The case study exposes a small semantic core from which
an application can make the choice with measurements and explicit
ownership rather than premature assumptions about what a database-shaped
language must do.

